<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stjepan Vukčić Kosača</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1404">1404</a>–<a href="/years/1466">1466</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stjepan Vukčić Kosača was the most powerful noble of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> in the mid-15th century, inheriting Hum and the coast from his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandalj-hranic-kosaca">Sandalj Hranić</a> in 1435. Often at odds with the Bosnian kings and Ragusa, in 1448 he took the title Herceg of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-sava">Saint Sava</a>, from which his lands took the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> conquered most of his territory before his death in 1466.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stjepan-vukcic-kosaca.jpg" alt="Stjepan Vukčić Kosača" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Matěj Baťha · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Blagaj_fort.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Stjepan was the son of Vukac Hranić Kosača, Knez of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drina">Drina</a>, and his wife Katarina, whose ancestry is unknown. His father held modest hereditary lands in the Upper Drina region in the east of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, around the village of Kosače near Goražde, which still bears the family name. With his father and his uncles Sandalj and Vuk he was admitted to the nobility of the Republic of Ragusa, and a charter of 30 October 1435 granted him and his sons Vladislav and Vlatko citizenship and a palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>. In 1424 he married Jelena Balšić, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balsa-iii">Balša III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Neighbours who expected the new head of the family to be weak eyed his inheritance, but in his first years he joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a>'s attack on the Serbian Despotate in 1439, took Omiš after an eight-month siege and probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poljica">Poljica</a>, pushed the Pavlović family out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebinje">Trebinje</a> and Dračevica, and seized Upper Zeta and Bar. After the death of King Tvrtko II in 1443 he opposed the new king, Thomas, and backed his exiled brother Radivoj, the Ottoman candidate, which began years of civil war. Thomas sought peace by marrying Stjepan's daughter Katarina in 1446.</p><p>In 1451 Stjepan attacked Ragusa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konavle">Konavle</a> and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a> in the Second Konavle War; the republic declared him a traitor and offered 15,000 ducats to anyone who killed him. A lifelong member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnian-church">Bosnian Church</a>, he sheltered at least 40 of its high clergy when King Thomas moved against its followers in 1459. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-sava">Saint Sava</a> of his title came from the relics of the Serbian saint kept at Mileševa in his lands. Reconciliation came only under Thomas's son, King Stephen Tomašević.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> in 1463, Mahmud Pasha besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blagaj">Blagaj</a>, and Stjepan ceded all his lands north of it for a truce. Old and terminally ill, he dictated his testament at Novi on 21 May 1466, blaming his eldest son Vladislav for bringing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> into Bosnia, and died the next day. His youngest son became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hersekzade-ahmed-pasha">Hersekzade Ahmed Pasha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-vizier">Grand Vizier</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>. The town of Novi, his winter seat, took his title as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herceg-novi">Herceg Novi</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stjepan Vukčić Kosača” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Stjepan Vukčić Kosača</b> inherited the lands of Hum and the coast from his uncle Sandalj Hranić in 1435, becoming the mightiest noble of the Kingdom of Bosnia.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saint-sava">Duchy of Saint Sava</a></li><li><blockquote>Royal power then weakened as great nobles such as Hrvoje Vukčić Hrvatinić and <b>Stjepan Vukčić Kosača</b>, whose lands became Herzegovina, made and unmade kings.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bosnia">Kingdom of Bosnia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Goražde, in what is now <a href="/countries/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herceg-novi">Herceg Novi</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.67,18.98&amp;year=1444" aria-label="Goražde on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5006 616 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Stjepan Vukčić Kosača"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5456 886h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5456 886h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.67,18.98&amp;year=1444">What was here in 1444? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
